## Try it on your PC

No hardware needed: [`examples/host`](examples/host/README.md) runs the driver against a simulated part.

```bash
cmake -S examples/host -B build-example && cmake --build build-example && ./build-example/xmpl100_host_example
```

## Use it on your MCU

**1. Wire it** ([Hardware](docs/hardware.md) has every pin and the timing):

| MCU | XMPL100 | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| GPIO input | OUT | Pull-up to 3.3 V (10 kΩ, or the MCU's internal one) |
| 3.3 V | VDD | 100 nF to GND close to the pin |
| GND | GND | |

**2. Write the port**: a class with `EnsureInitialized()` (configure the pins), `GpioRead()` (the level of OUT)
and `DelayUs()`, about 20 lines on most SDKs. [Porting](docs/porting.md) has the contract and a complete example.

**3. Read the switch:**

